  {
    "episode_id": 95390,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95390.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 48,
    "turn_text": "Yeah, well, glad to be back with you. You know, it's no coincidence that we filed the lawsuit on January 19th. That's because of the Trump administration, you know, in the rush to finalize various rules. That's when those rules became effective. So we sued a number of agencies and the secretaries who are in charge of those. That includes health and human services, USDA, housing and urban development over new rules that they implemented that became effective on January 19th, that really would allow for increased discrimination in social services programs. So things like food programs, health programs where the federal government is using federal money and that money then goes to like faith-based groups or private groups that provide services oftentimes to low-income people. And so the rules have changed substantially and would allow for greater discrimination. And one of the things I think is not insidious is that they no longer are requiring these providers to provide notice stuff to rule. So, you know, normally if people receive, let's say, food as part of a USDA food program, they might get a notice of their rights and it would say, you know, you can't be discriminated on a number of basis, including oftentimes a religion because that's a concern with a base provider. So now the problem is people don't even know that there are some rules that still exist and that providers can't use federal money to discriminate.",
