    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/5/95390.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 52,
    "turn_text": "that's right you know there was uh... and involved huge amount, we're stilling eight different agencies. So there's a lot of programs that this involves. And in 2016, before Obama left office, there was a lot of dialogue and changes to rules to basically try to balance the idea between private providers and people receiving vulnerable populations receiving benefits. And so part of those discussions then led with this idea, there's going to be a notice requirement. uh... one of those would be you know religious providers can't be using the federal money to do religious activities or course participation religious activity so if they're providing a federally funded meal you can't make people pray or or go to church in order to get that you know that that's not a use of federal dollars that's acceptable and it's misusing federal money on for religious purposes of that was kind of uh... some of the ideas behind the twenty sixteen rules which seem to have worked on And then in, you know, prior to leaving office, these 2020 rules, you know, really turn back the clock and make it, um, bend everything in favor of religious providers in terms of, you know, no, not only not providing the notice or potentially discrimination, they have an indirect aid programs, which are more like a voucher program. They can engage in religious activities, which wasn't permitted before. So there's really a, um, broad concern, again, because of all the number of programs that, you know, religious minorities, like, or atheists, are going to be coerced into religion, or this money is going to be, you know, misappropriated to further the religious goals of some of the organizations, which it really should be providing a service to people and not, you know, pushing religion on them.",
